<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.1: Evaluating Quadratic Functions in the Real World</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01989</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.1: Evaluating Quadratic Functions in the Real World</md:title>
    <md:uuid>12f98d21-ab27-4900-b004-4fa5e261b432</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>The height, in inches, of a frog's jump is modeled by the equation \(h(t)=60t-75t^2\), where the time \(t\), after the frog jumps, is measured in seconds.</para>
<para><figure><media alt="close-up of a frog on plant stem"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/dc7c6fde68fd9c5493e88f22a1997baa7fa2c8c5"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>For \(h(t)=60t-75t^2\):</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding"><title>Warm Up Activity</title><exercise data-content-id="67eec057-f46f-4f3c-887c-aad2d0a7c8a2"><problem><para>
      <para> Find \(h(0)\). </para>
        <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>0</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 0. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What do these values of \(h(0)\) and \(h(0.8)\) mean in terms of the frog's jump?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>They mean that the frog is on the ground when \(t=0\) (before jumping) and when \(t=0.8\) (when the frog lands on the ground).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="1fe24a64-efa0-46d4-9188-efa86198aa89"><problem><para>
      <para>After it jumped, how much time passed, in seconds, before the frog reached its maximum height?</para>
      <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
    </para></problem><solution><para>0.4</para><para>
      <para>The correct answer is 0.4 seconds. </para>
    </para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Explain how you know this is the time the frog reached the maximum height. </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> The frog reached its maximum height at \(t=0.4\), as that is the halfway point of the jump. If we graph the function, the vertex of the graph will have a horizontal coordinate (\(t\)-coordinate) of 0.4.</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
</content>
